<UserControl xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:panel="clr-namespace:D47.App.Panel"
             xmlns:controls="clr-namespace:D47.App.Controls"
             x:Class="D47.App.Panel.PanelView"
             x:DataType="panel:PanelViewModel"
             Background="{DynamicResource D47.Background}"
             Foreground="{DynamicResource D47.Text}">

  <!--
    One view definition, instantiated twice: once by the desktop window, once by the VR
    overlay, both bound to one PanelViewModel. A Visual belongs to exactly one visual tree so
    the framework will not render a single instance to two targets, but the constraint the
    checklist is protecting is satisfied either way - there is no second UI codebase and no
    path where the VR surface is a screenshot of the desktop one (architecture.md D1).

    Nothing here knows about a window. That is what makes the overlay path minimise-safe: the
    window the VR surface hosts this in is constructed and never shown, so there is no window
    state for a minimise to put it in.

    Mini is a mode of this panel, not another one. The rows mini does without bind IsFull.

    Colour by role, never by literal: every brush is a theme resource, so switching theme
    repaints this without it knowing a theme exists (list.md Phase 4).
  -->
  <!--
    A surface the pointer goes straight through draws no buttons (asked for 2026-08-24: "clickable
    controls should be removed from the 2D overlay - nothing can be clicked, and it makes more room
    for the data").

    A style rather than a pass over each page, because the pages are furnished by hosts and there
    are five of them: the checklist's filter and Add, the engineer pages, the clocks. One selector
    reaches all of them and reaches the next one nobody has written yet.

    <b>Exact `Button`, deliberately.</b> Avalonia matches the type rather than the hierarchy unless
    asked with `:is()`, and that is the distinction wanted here: a `Button` exists to be pressed and
    is nothing without a pointer, while `CheckBox` shows whether a checklist line is done and
    `RepeatButton` is half of a scrollbar. Removing those would take away the data rather than make
    room for it.
  -->
  <UserControl.Styles>
    <Style Selector="panel|PanelView.output-only Button">
      <Setter Property="IsVisible" Value="False" />
    </Style>
  </UserControl.Styles>

  <UserControl.Resources>
    <ResourceDictionary>
      <ResourceDictionary.MergedDictionaries>
        <ResourceInclude Source="avares://d47/Panel/PanelTabs.axaml" />
      </ResourceDictionary.MergedDictionaries>
    </ResourceDictionary>
  </UserControl.Resources>

  <DockPanel Margin="24,16,24,24">

    <!--
      LastChildFill is off because there is no last child to fill with: the version line that
      used to sit on the left has moved to the title bar, and without this the final docked
      button silently stops docking and stretches across the header instead.
    -->
    <DockPanel x:Name="Header" DockPanel.Dock="Top" LastChildFill="False">
      <!--
        Help. The gear used to sit beside it and no longer does: settings is a page of this panel
        now, chosen from the tab strip below like every other page, and a second affordance
        opening the same page would be a second thing to keep in step (list.md Phase 12).

        Drawn rather than typed. A "?" set as text was a character in a font: lighter than the
        glyph beside it, and sitting on a baseline rather than in the middle of its box, so it
        read as smaller and hung high. This is a 24-unit coordinate space scaled into a
        17-pixel box, so it lines up by construction rather than by adjustment.

        Stroked rather than filled, because thickness is the property being matched and a stroke
        states it directly. The dot is a zero-length segment with a round cap - a line from a
        point to itself, drawn as a disc exactly as wide as the stroke, which keeps it in step
        with the hook for free.
      -->
      <!--
        Square, with the glyph centred in it by construction (remediation.md 10, item 4).
        <para/>
        It was a uniform padding around a 17x17 Path, which reads as centred and is not. Two things
        were off. The button was as wide as the glyph plus its padding and as tall as the glyph
        plus the same, but the ink is a question mark — about half as wide as it is tall — and
        <c>Stretch="Uniform"</c> scales the geometry's own bounds, so the drawn mark filled the
        height and left slack across the width that the padding then did not account for. And the
        button's content alignment was whatever the default theme said rather than centre.
        <para/>
        So the button is a fixed square, the content is centred on both axes explicitly, and the
        Path's box is the shape of the ink rather than a square the ink sits inside. The highlight
        is the button's own background, so a glyph centred in the button is centred in the
        highlight — which is what was being asked for.
      -->
      <!--
        Hidden until the host hands this surface a way to open the site (EnableHelp). The headset
        copy is never handed one: the button opened a browser on the desktop, which a Commander in
        the headset cannot see (change-requests.md 24).
      -->
      <!--
        The pre-release mark (#92). Docked right so it sits immediately left of the help mark, and
        hidden by default for the same reason the help button is: a local IsVisible outranks a
        style setter, so this one is furnished by hand and is off on the headset's copy along with
        the button it sits beside. A Commander in a headset is flying, and a badge about which
        build they are on is chrome they cannot dismiss and did not ask for.
      -->
      <Border x:Name="PreReleaseBadge"
              DockPanel.Dock="Right"
              IsVisible="False"
              VerticalAlignment="Top"
              Margin="0,9,6,0"
              Padding="7,2"
              CornerRadius="3"
              Background="Transparent"
              BorderThickness="1"
              BorderBrush="{DynamicResource D47.TextMuted}"
              ToolTip.Tip="This is a pre-release. It is offered to nobody automatically, and it is not final.">
        <TextBlock x:Name="PreReleaseBadgeText"
                   Text="PRE-RELEASE"
                   FontSize="{StaticResource D47.Type.Small}"
                   FontWeight="Bold"
                   Foreground="{DynamicResource D47.TextMuted}" />
      </Border>

      <Button x:Name="HelpButton"
              DockPanel.Dock="Right"
              IsVisible="False"
              Width="35"
              Height="35"
              Padding="0"
              HorizontalContentAlignment="Center"
              VerticalContentAlignment="Center"
              VerticalAlignment="Top"
              CornerRadius="4"
              Background="Transparent"
              BorderThickness="0"
              ToolTip.Tip="Open the documentation"
              Click="OnHelpClick"
              PointerEntered="OnHelpPointerEntered"
              PointerExited="OnHelpPointerExited">
        <!--
          Width is the ink's own aspect rather than a square: the geometry spans 9.2 units across
          and 18.6 down, so 8.4 by 17 is the box it actually fills. A 17-wide box would leave four
          pixels of nothing on each side of the mark and hang it left of centre.
        -->
        <Path x:Name="HelpGlyph"
              Width="8.4" Height="17"
              Stretch="Uniform"
              HorizontalAlignment="Center"
              VerticalAlignment="Center"
              Stroke="{DynamicResource D47.TextMuted}"
              StrokeThickness="2.8"
              StrokeLineCap="Round"
              StrokeJoin="Round"
              Data="M 7.4,7.6 A 4.6,4.6 0 1 1 12,12.2 L 12,15.5 M 12,20.2 L 12,20.2" />
      </Button>

      <!--
        The ship AI's face, on the left, where the build line used to be. It earns that space in
        a way a version string never did: it is the one thing on the panel that says what D47 is
        doing right now without the Commander reading anything, which is the whole point in a
        cockpit. Drawn rather than decoded — see AvatarView for why that is not an aesthetic
        choice — and present in mini too, since a reduced content set is exactly where a glance
        has to carry more.
      -->
      <panel:AvatarView x:Name="Avatar"
                        DockPanel.Dock="Left"
                        VerticalAlignment="Top"
                        Margin="0,0,10,0" />
    </DockPanel>

    <!-- Both banners in one region, so mini hides them with one line and each still decides
         for itself whether it has anything to say. -->
    <StackPanel x:Name="Banners" DockPanel.Dock="Top">

    <!-- Settings that failed to load are said out loud, not swallowed. Dismissable, because a
         fault the Commander has read and decided about should not go on taking a strip off the
         top of the panel for the rest of the session — and the same fault arising again raises
         it again, so dismissing hides a message rather than muting a problem. -->
    <Border x:Name="ErrorBanner"
            IsVisible="{Binding HasError}"
            Margin="0,14,0,0"
            Padding="12"
            CornerRadius="4"
            Background="{DynamicResource D47.SurfaceAlt}"
            BorderBrush="{DynamicResource D47.Danger}"
            BorderThickness="1">
      <DockPanel>
        <Button x:Name="DismissErrorButton"
                DockPanel.Dock="Right"
                Margin="10,0,0,0"
                Padding="7,1"
                VerticalAlignment="Top"
                Background="Transparent"
                BorderThickness="0"
                Foreground="{DynamicResource D47.Danger}"
                FontSize="{StaticResource D47.Type.Body}"
                Content="&#x2715;"
                ToolTip.Tip="Dismiss"
                Click="OnDismissErrorClick" />
        <SelectableTextBlock x:Name="ErrorText"
                             Text="{Binding ErrorText}"
                             TextWrapping="Wrap"
                             FontSize="{StaticResource D47.Type.Body}"
                             Foreground="{DynamicResource D47.Danger}" />
      </DockPanel>
    </Border>

    <!-- Hidden until a background check finds a newer release; never blocks startup. -->
    <Border x:Name="UpdateBanner"
            IsVisible="{Binding HasUpdate}"
            Margin="0,14,0,0"
            Padding="12"
            CornerRadius="4"
            Background="{DynamicResource D47.SurfaceAlt}"
            BorderBrush="{DynamicResource D47.Info}"
            BorderThickness="1">
      <DockPanel>
        <StackPanel DockPanel.Dock="Right"
                    Orientation="Horizontal"
                    Spacing="8"
                    IsVisible="{Binding UpdateActionable}">
          <Button x:Name="UpdateNowButton" Content="Update now" Click="OnUpdateNowClick" />
          <Button x:Name="UpdateLaterButton" Content="Later" Click="OnUpdateLaterClick" />
        </StackPanel>
        <TextBlock x:Name="UpdateText"
                   Text="{Binding UpdateText}"
                   TextWrapping="Wrap"
                   FontSize="{StaticResource D47.Type.Body}"
                   VerticalAlignment="Center" />
      </DockPanel>
    </Border>


    </StackPanel>

    <!-- Ask box. Enter sends; the button is for discoverability. -->
    <Border x:Name="AskRow" DockPanel.Dock="Bottom" Margin="0,14,0,0">
      <DockPanel>
        <!-- The send glyph, drawn in-repo like the help mark. The label is gone because a paper
             aeroplane next to a text box needs no caption anywhere a Commander has been. -->
        <Button x:Name="AskButton"
                DockPanel.Dock="Right"
                Margin="8,0,0,0"
                Padding="11,7"
                CornerRadius="4"
                IsEnabled="{Binding CanAsk}"
                Background="{DynamicResource D47.SurfaceAlt}"
                BorderBrush="{DynamicResource D47.Border}"
                BorderThickness="1"
                ToolTip.Tip="Send (Enter)"
                Click="OnAskClick">
          <Path x:Name="AskGlyph"
                Width="17" Height="17"
                Stretch="Uniform"
                Fill="{DynamicResource D47.Accent}"
                Data="M 2.5,3 L 22,12 L 2.5,21 L 2.5,13.6 L 14.5,12 L 2.5,10.4 Z" />
        </Button>
        <!-- The placeholder is set in code rather than bound; see ApplyAskHint for why a
             binding on this particular control is the wrong tool. -->
        <TextBox x:Name="AskBox"
                 Text="{Binding AskText, Mode=TwoWay}"
                 KeyDown="OnAskBoxKeyDown" />
      </DockPanel>
    </Border>

    <!--
      The provenance line, and beside it the one thing on this panel that is about right now
      rather than about the last turn: whether the microphone is open (list.md Phase 13).

      It sits in this row rather than in the header because the header is what mini takes away,
      and mini is the headset's normal state. "Continuous capture with no visible state is the
      thing a user is right to distrust" does not become less true at a metre — so the indicator
      lives in the one region both surfaces show in both modes.

      Docked left of the turn line rather than under it: the two are read at different moments
      and neither should push the other around as it changes length.
    -->
    <DockPanel x:Name="StatusRow" DockPanel.Dock="Bottom" Margin="0,10,0,0">

      <Border x:Name="MicrophoneRow"
              DockPanel.Dock="Left"
              IsVisible="{Binding MicrophoneVisible}"
              Margin="0,0,10,0"
              Padding="7,2"
              CornerRadius="3"
              VerticalAlignment="Center"
              Background="{DynamicResource D47.SurfaceAlt}"
              BorderThickness="1">
        <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="6">
          <!--
            A microphone, drawn rather than typed, for the reason the help mark already is: a
            glyph taken from a font is whatever weight that font happens to have, and it hangs
            off a baseline instead of sitting in the middle of its box. A 24-unit coordinate
            space scaled into a 13-pixel box lines up with the text beside it by construction.

            Filled or hollow is the state, not just the colour, because a Commander glancing at
            this in a headset is reading a shape before they read a hue - and because a colour
            difference alone is not a difference for everybody.
          -->
          <Path x:Name="MicrophoneGlyph"
                Width="13" Height="13"
                VerticalAlignment="Center"
                Stretch="Uniform"
                StrokeThickness="2"
                StrokeLineCap="Round"
                StrokeJoin="Round"
                Data="M 12,3 A 3,3 0 0 1 15,6 L 15,11 A 3,3 0 0 1 9,11 L 9,6 A 3,3 0 0 1 12,3 Z
                      M 5.5,10.5 A 6.5,6.5 0 0 0 18.5,10.5 M 12,17 L 12,21" />
          <TextBlock x:Name="MicrophoneLabel"
                     VerticalAlignment="Center"
                     FontSize="{StaticResource D47.Type.Small}" />
        </StackPanel>
      </Border>

      <!--
        Which assigned HOTAS switches currently sit against the game's state (list.md Phase 21).
        In this row rather than in the banners, and the difference is the whole point: mini hides
        the banners and mini is the headset's normal state, so a switch annunciator up there
        would be invisible exactly where a Commander cannot see their own switches either.

        Docked right, so it never pushes the microphone indicator around — the two change on
        completely different clocks.
      -->
      <Border x:Name="SwitchRow"
              DockPanel.Dock="Right"
              IsVisible="{Binding SwitchesVisible}"
              Margin="10,0,0,0"
              Padding="7,2"
              CornerRadius="3"
              VerticalAlignment="Center"
              Background="{DynamicResource D47.SurfaceAlt}"
              BorderBrush="{DynamicResource D47.Accent}"
              BorderThickness="1">
        <TextBlock x:Name="SwitchText"
                   Text="{Binding SwitchesText}"
                   VerticalAlignment="Center"
                   TextWrapping="Wrap"
                   FontSize="{StaticResource D47.Type.Small}"
                   Foreground="{DynamicResource D47.Accent}" />
      </Border>

      <!--
        One line of provenance per turn: which path answered, at what effort, for what.

        The figures behind it live in a dialog rather than on the line. Eleven numbers on one
        row beside a running game is a wall a Commander reads none of; the ones that answer
        "did that work" stay here and the rest are a click away. The link is docked so the line
        wraps against it rather than pushing it off the row.
      -->
      <Button x:Name="TurnDetails"
              DockPanel.Dock="Right"
              Content="Details"
              IsVisible="False"
              Margin="8,0,0,0"
              Padding="6,1"
              MinWidth="0"
              VerticalAlignment="Center"
              Background="Transparent"
              BorderThickness="0"
              Cursor="Hand"
              FontSize="{StaticResource D47.Type.Secondary}"
              Foreground="{DynamicResource D47.Accent}"
              ToolTip.Tip="Tokens, cost, and what this has come to over time"
              Click="OnTurnDetailsClick" />
      <TextBlock x:Name="TurnLine"
                 Text="{Binding TurnLine}"
                 VerticalAlignment="Center"
                 FontFamily="Cascadia Mono,Consolas,monospace"
                 FontSize="{StaticResource D47.Type.Secondary}"
                 Foreground="{DynamicResource D47.TextMuted}"
                 TextWrapping="Wrap" />
    </DockPanel>

    <!--
      The way out of mini, drawn (asked for 2026-08-24). Phase 51 ruled that the way back must not
      live in the thing that disappears and listed three that do not: the hotkey, the phrase and
      the title bar. The Commander's answer on meeting it was that a surface with no visible
      control to leave by is one you have to be told about — so there is one, and the other three
      stand beside it rather than being replaced.

      Its own row rather than a seat in StatusRow, because StatusRow is the transcript's: it
      carries the provenance line and the microphone, and it is hidden on any other tab. A way out
      that vanished when mini was showing the story would be a way out with a hole in it.

      Hidden unless a host furnishes it, exactly as TurnDetails above is — so the headset's mini is
      untouched and the flat overlay, which the pointer goes straight through, does not draw a
      button nobody could press.
    -->
    <DockPanel x:Name="ModeRow" DockPanel.Dock="Bottom" IsVisible="False" Margin="0,8,0,0">
      <Button x:Name="ModeToggle"
              DockPanel.Dock="Right"
              Padding="6,1"
              MinWidth="0"
              VerticalAlignment="Center"
              Background="Transparent"
              BorderThickness="0"
              Cursor="Hand"
              FontSize="{StaticResource D47.Type.Secondary}"
              Foreground="{DynamicResource D47.Accent}"
              Click="OnModeToggleClick" />
      <TextBlock />
    </DockPanel>

    <!--
      Which surface of the panel this instantiation is showing, and which reading of it. On the
      view rather than on the view model, for the same reason PanelMode is: both surfaces bind
      one model, so a page held there would send the headset to the log file the moment the
      window went to it. The model owns the content; how much of it a given surface shows is
      that surface's business.
    -->
    <DockPanel x:Name="TabStrip" DockPanel.Dock="Top" Margin="0,16,0,0">

      <!--
        The steppers, for when the tabs do not fit (remediation.md 10, item 1).
        <para/>
        The strip used to carry the tabs, the mode control and the search box in one row, and
        below a certain width they simply overlapped: a DockPanel gives its docked children what
        they ask for and lets the filling child have whatever is left, which below a threshold is
        a negative number rather than a scrollbar. The mode control and the search box have moved
        into the pane, which is most of the fix; these are what makes the remainder reachable at
        any width at all, including the headset's, where the strip is narrower still.
        <para/>
        Docked outside the scroller rather than floating over it, so a tab is never underneath the
        control that scrolls to it. Hidden entirely when everything fits, which is the ordinary
        case on a desktop — a pair of dead arrows either side of six tabs is two controls that do
        nothing, permanently.
      -->
      <Button x:Name="TabsLeft"
              DockPanel.Dock="Left"
              Content="&#x2039;"
              IsVisible="False"
              Padding="7,0"
              MinWidth="0"
              Height="30"
              Margin="0,0,4,0"
              VerticalAlignment="Bottom"
              FontSize="{StaticResource D47.Type.Body}"
              ToolTip.Tip="Earlier tabs"
              AutomationProperties.Name="Show earlier tabs"
              Click="OnTabsLeftClick" />
      <Button x:Name="TabsRight"
              DockPanel.Dock="Right"
              Content="&#x203A;"
              IsVisible="False"
              Padding="7,0"
              MinWidth="0"
              Height="30"
              Margin="4,0,0,0"
              VerticalAlignment="Bottom"
              FontSize="{StaticResource D47.Type.Body}"
              ToolTip.Tip="Later tabs"
              AutomationProperties.Name="Show later tabs"
              Click="OnTabsRightClick" />

      <!--
        The strip itself, which scrolls rather than wraps. Wrapping would move the tabs into a
        second row and take the join to the panel with them, and the join is the whole idiom —
        a tab that is not touching its page does not read as a tab.

        No visible scrollbar: a horizontal bar under a tab strip reads as a second, thinner strip.
        The arrows above are the affordance, and the wheel and a drag still work.
      -->
      <ScrollViewer x:Name="TabsScroller"
                    HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Hidden"
                    VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled"
                    SizeChanged="OnTabsResized">

      <!--
        No GroupName, deliberately. Avalonia groups named radio buttons across the whole
        application rather than within a tree, and this view is instantiated twice - once by the
        window, once by the headset overlay. A shared name put both strips in one group, so the
        overlay's tabs unchecked the window's as they were built, and the window opened with no
        tab highlighted until it was clicked. Nameless, they group by their parent panel, which
        is one strip per surface - which is what a tab strip is.

        Six tabs, and five in the headset: Settings stays hidden there for the reason recorded
        below. Every tab but Transcript is hidden until a host furnishes it, which is what lets
        the phases that fill them land one at a time without a dead tab in between - the same
        rule Settings has followed since Phase 12, generalised.
      -->
      <StackPanel x:Name="Tabs"
                  Orientation="Horizontal"
                  VerticalAlignment="Bottom">
        <!--
          One tab for three readings of one exchange. Conversation and Technical are the same
          turns at two verbosities and the log is a file read off disk - which is why the busy
          glyph rides the mode rather than the tab, and why the collapse is three modes rather
          than a single toggle (list.md Phase 25).
        -->
        <RadioButton x:Name="TranscriptTab"
                     Theme="{StaticResource D47.Tab}"
                     IsChecked="True"
                     Content="Transcript"
                     Tapped="OnTabTapped"
                     IsCheckedChanged="OnTabChecked" />
        <!--
          Where the Commander is going, in three readings of one journey (list.md Phase 37).
          Beside the transcript rather than out among the ledgers: a route is read while flying,
          and the tab a hand reaches for in flight should not be the sixth one along.
        -->
        <RadioButton x:Name="RoutingTab"
                     Theme="{StaticResource D47.Tab}"
                     IsVisible="False"
                     Content="Routing"
                     Tapped="OnTabTapped"
                     IsCheckedChanged="OnTabChecked" />
        <RadioButton x:Name="ChecklistTab"
                     Theme="{StaticResource D47.Tab}"
                     IsVisible="False"
                     Content="Checklist"
                     Tapped="OnTabTapped"
                     IsCheckedChanged="OnTabChecked" />
        <RadioButton x:Name="LoadoutTab"
                     Theme="{StaticResource D47.Tab}"
                     IsVisible="False"
                     Content="Loadout"
                     Tapped="OnTabTapped"
                     IsCheckedChanged="OnTabChecked" />
        <RadioButton x:Name="EngineersTab"
                     Theme="{StaticResource D47.Tab}"
                     IsVisible="False"
                     Content="Engineers"
                     Tapped="OnTabTapped"
                     IsCheckedChanged="OnTabChecked" />
        <!--
          Stories the Commander flies (list.md Phase 47). After Engineers on the Commander's
          instruction, 2026-08-22: a story turns out to be read between trips rather than during
          one, which is where the ledgers are.
        -->
        <RadioButton x:Name="AdventuresTab"
                     Theme="{StaticResource D47.Tab}"
                     IsVisible="False"
                     Content="Adventures"
                     Tapped="OnTabTapped"
                     IsCheckedChanged="OnTabChecked" />
        <RadioButton x:Name="UtilitiesTab"
                     Theme="{StaticResource D47.Tab}"
                     IsVisible="False"
                     Content="Utilities"
                     Tapped="OnTabTapped"
                     IsCheckedChanged="OnTabChecked" />
        <!--
          Hidden until a host says otherwise, and only the desktop window ever does. The headset
          renders this same view, and a 1180-pixel nav column at a metre is not a settings surface
          so much as a wall - so the tab is absent there rather than present and asking to be
          remembered about (list.md Phase 12).
        -->
        <RadioButton x:Name="SettingsTab"
                     Theme="{StaticResource D47.Tab.Settings}"
                     IsVisible="False"
                     Content="Settings"
                     Tapped="OnTabTapped"
                     IsCheckedChanged="OnTabChecked" />
      </StackPanel>

      </ScrollViewer>

    </DockPanel>

    <!--
      Where the Commander is, once they are below a root (list.md Phase 25, "Drill in, and find
      your way back").

      Both the back affordance and the you-are-here, because a headset has no title bar to
      orient by and losing your place is expensive when you cannot glance at a second monitor.
      The first crumb is the tab itself - the tab is the root rather than the first level - so
      this row says nothing at a root and is not drawn there, which is also what keeps it from
      reading as a second strip beside the mode control it replaces.

      Filled in code from the navigator's trail, because a trail can materialise mid-drill: a
      spoken "open the third weapon slot on the Corsair" lands three levels down and the row has
      to be right the first time it is drawn.
    -->
    <StackPanel x:Name="CrumbRow"
                DockPanel.Dock="Top"
                Orientation="Horizontal"
                IsVisible="False"
                Margin="0,10,0,0"
                Spacing="0" />

    <!--
      One slot, every page. The transcript and each furnished tab occupy the same place under
      the strip and are swapped by which tab is checked, which is what makes settings a page of
      this window rather than a second window - and what makes every surface Phases 24 and 26-28
      add a page of it too, on the same terms.
    -->
    <!--
      One bordered container for the whole content region, with the page's own controls inside it
      (remediation.md 10, items 1, 2 and 3).
      <para/>
      It used to be two borders side by side in a Panel — one for the transcript, one for every
      furnished page — because the two want different fills: the transcript is Surface and the
      settings page is Background, since a card the colour of its page is not a card. That is
      still true and is still the rule; what changed is that one border can carry either fill, so
      the difference is a property rather than a second control. ApplyChrome sets it.
      <para/>
      Top-left corner square, because a tab is sitting on it. Rounding it there would leave a
      visible notch between the first tab and the panel it belongs to.
    -->
    <Border x:Name="ContentPane"
            BorderBrush="{DynamicResource D47.Border}"
            BorderThickness="1"
            CornerRadius="0,4,4,4">
      <DockPanel>

      <!--
        The page's own bar: which reading of the page is showing, and the two things that act on
        it (remediation.md 10, items 1, 2 and 3).
        <para/>
        <b>Inside the panel rather than beside the tabs.</b> The mode control was a segmented pill
        riding in the tab strip's own row, on the argument that two stacked strips read worse than
        eight flat tabs. That argument was about a second strip of <em>tabs</em>; what it produced
        was a row with three unrelated controls competing for one width, which below a certain
        window size stopped being a layout at all. A control that says which reading of this page
        you are looking at belongs to the page, and a page is what is inside the border.
        <para/>
        And it is a drop-down now rather than a row of segments, so it costs one control's width
        whatever the tab offers — three readings or six.
      -->
      <DockPanel x:Name="PageBar"
                 DockPanel.Dock="Top"
                 IsVisible="False"
                 Margin="14,12,14,0">
        <!--
          Which reading of this page is showing. A button rather than a ComboBox, because a
          ComboBox is a popup and a popup has no top level to hang from on a window that is never
          shown — the headset's host is exactly that. It opens the panel's own chooser in the
          layer, like every other choice here.

          Filled in code, because the roots come from the navigator: a tab declares its own, and a
          tab with one root shows no control at all. Hidden below the root, where the breadcrumb
          is already answering which stack you are in.
        -->
        <Button x:Name="ModeButton"
                DockPanel.Dock="Left"
                Margin="0,0,12,0"
                VerticalAlignment="Center"
                Height="28"
                Padding="10,0"
                CornerRadius="4"
                IsVisible="False"
                FontSize="{StaticResource D47.Type.Secondary}"
                Click="OnModeClick" />
        <!--
          One search affordance for the surface, on the tab that is selected — not four features
          bolted to four pages (list.md Phase 12). What a match does differs by page and that
          difference is the design: the transcript pages highlight and step, and Settings filters,
          so the count and the steppers are hidden where they would mean nothing.

          Hidden until a host enables it, which only the desktop window does. Mini shows no strip
          and the headset has no keyboard, and a search box the Commander cannot type into is
          worse than no search at all.
        -->
        <!--
          A DockPanel rather than a horizontal stack, and the search box is the child that flexes
          (remediation.md 10, item 1). A stack with a fixed 200-pixel box in it takes the width it
          wants and leaves the mode button beside it whatever is left, which on a narrow pane is a
          sliver — the same failure this batch removed from the tab strip, reproduced one row
          down. Everything here keeps its size and the box gives, because a search box that is
          narrower is still a search box and a mode button that is four pixels wide is not a
          control at all.
        -->
        <DockPanel x:Name="SearchRow"
                   LastChildFill="True"
                   IsVisible="False"
                   VerticalAlignment="Center"
                   Margin="12,0,0,0">
          <!--
            One affordance for the whole page as it is currently shown (list.md Phase 19, "Copy
            log"). Beside the search box because both are about the page rather than about the
            conversation, and because this is the row a Commander looks at when they are reading
            rather than talking. The tedious case is several hundred lines of a log, to paste into
            an issue.

            "Copy All", not "Copy" (remediation.md 10, item 3). The text on this page is selectable
            and Ctrl+C already works on a selection, so a button beside it saying "Copy" reads as
            copying that selection — which is the one thing it does not do.

            Centred on both axes, and the content alignment is the half that was missing: the
            height made the button the right size and the label still sat where the default put
            it, which beside a 28-pixel search box reads as one control hanging lower than its
            neighbours.
          -->
          <Button x:Name="CopyButton"
                  DockPanel.Dock="Left"
                  Margin="0,0,6,0"
                  Height="28"
                  VerticalAlignment="Center"
                  VerticalContentAlignment="Center"
                  HorizontalContentAlignment="Center"
                  Padding="10,0"
                  FontSize="{StaticResource D47.Type.Secondary}"
                  ToolTip.Tip="Copy this whole page to the clipboard"
                  Click="OnCopyClick" />
          <!--
            The other thing a Commander does with these two pages, and the one Copy All cannot do
            (#160). Copy hands over the page as it stands — the Commander's name, other people's
            messages, a screenful of whatever happened to be on screen. This cuts a window around
            an incident, scrubs it, and shows exactly what would leave before anything does.

            Worded rather than a glyph, unlike Copy beside it. Copy is a verb everybody already
            knows and a clipboard icon says it; this is a thing nobody has done before and an icon
            for it would be a small picture nobody presses. Shown only on the two pages it can cut
            from, and only where a host has wired it — the headset has no clipboard and no file
            picker, so on that surface it is simply absent.
          -->
          <Button x:Name="DonateButton"
                  DockPanel.Dock="Left"
                  Margin="0,0,6,0"
                  Height="28"
                  VerticalAlignment="Center"
                  VerticalContentAlignment="Center"
                  HorizontalContentAlignment="Center"
                  Padding="10,0"
                  IsVisible="False"
                  Content="Donate excerpt…"
                  FontSize="{StaticResource D47.Type.Secondary}"
                  ToolTip.Tip="Cut the journal and log around an incident, scrubbed, to attach to a bug report"
                  Click="OnDonateClick" />
          <!--
            The clear affordance sits inside the field, not beside it. Another sibling in this
            StackPanel would sit outside the border and read as a fourth button in a row that
            already has Copy and the two steppers, rather than as part of the box it empties.

            U+00D7, the multiplication sign, and deliberately not the letter x — the same reason
            the steppers above are U+2039 and U+203A rather than angle brackets.

            Hidden until there is something to clear, or it is a control that does nothing for
            most of the time a Commander can see it.
          -->
          <!--
            Docked innermost-last, and the box is the last child so it is the one that fills.
            Order is load-bearing twice over. A DockPanel gives its first right-docked child the
            rightmost slot, so the trio is declared next, previous, count to read left to right as
            count, previous, next. And LastChildFill overrides the last child's own Dock: with the
            steppers declared last, one of them stretched across the whole row and the count sat
            outside it (remediation.md 11, item 2).
          -->
          <Button x:Name="SearchNext"
                  DockPanel.Dock="Right"
                  Margin="6,0,0,0"
                  Content="&#x203A;"
                  Padding="8,0"
                  MinWidth="0"
                  Height="28"
                  IsVisible="False"
                  FontSize="{StaticResource D47.Type.Body}"
                  ToolTip.Tip="Next match (Enter)"
                  Click="OnSearchNextClick" />
          <Button x:Name="SearchPrevious"
                  DockPanel.Dock="Right"
                  Margin="6,0,0,0"
                  Content="&#x2039;"
                  Padding="8,0"
                  MinWidth="0"
                  Height="28"
                  IsVisible="False"
                  FontSize="{StaticResource D47.Type.Body}"
                  ToolTip.Tip="Previous match (Shift+Enter)"
                  Click="OnSearchPreviousClick" />
          <TextBlock x:Name="SearchCount"
                     DockPanel.Dock="Right"
                     Margin="6,0,0,0"
                     VerticalAlignment="Center"
                     IsVisible="False"
                     FontSize="{StaticResource D47.Type.Secondary}"
                     Foreground="{DynamicResource D47.TextMuted}" />
          <!--
            Body, not Secondary. A field the Commander types into is not supporting text, and at
            13 beside a page of 14 it read as a smaller-than-everything control rather than as a
            quieter one — which is the exact drift TypeScale exists to stop.
          -->
          <!--
            The child that gives, and the only one that can. It takes whatever width the row has
            spare, so no other control has to hold a gap — which is the whole of item 2: the slack
            has to go somewhere, and every other control here is the wrong place for it.
            <para/>
            Capping it and right-aligning the group was tried and does not work: a stretched child
            with a MaxWidth is centred in what is left, which puts a gap on both sides of the box
            instead of one beside it, and a child aligned Right does not stretch at all — it
            collapses to MinWidth at every window size. So the box is wide on a wide window, which
            is a cosmetic cost paid for having no gap at any width and still shrinking to 90 on a
            narrow pane.
          -->
          <TextBox x:Name="SearchInput"
                   MinWidth="90"
                   Height="28"
                   Padding="8,3"
                   FontSize="{StaticResource D47.Type.Body}"
                   VerticalContentAlignment="Center"
                   PlaceholderText="Search this page"
                   TextChanged="OnSearchChanged"
                   KeyDown="OnSearchKeyDown">
            <TextBox.InnerRightContent>
              <Button x:Name="SearchClear"
                      Content="&#x00D7;"
                      Padding="6,0"
                      MinWidth="0"
                      Margin="0,0,2,0"
                      IsVisible="False"
                      Background="Transparent"
                      BorderThickness="0"
                      VerticalAlignment="Center"
                      FontSize="{StaticResource D47.Type.Body}"
                      Foreground="{DynamicResource D47.TextMuted}"
                      Cursor="Hand"
                      ToolTip.Tip="Clear the search (Esc)"
                      AutomationProperties.Name="Clear the search"
                      Click="OnSearchClearClick" />
            </TextBox.InnerRightContent>
          </TextBox>
        </DockPanel>
      </DockPanel>

      <Panel>
      <Border x:Name="TranscriptPane"
              Padding="14">
        <Panel>
          <ScrollViewer x:Name="TranscriptScroller" ScrollChanged="OnTranscriptScrolled">
            <!--
              Two presentations of one page, and exactly one of them is ever visible. The
              conversation is a conversation, so it is drawn as one - each turn its own bubble,
              the Commander's on the right (asked for 2026-08-22). Technical and the log file
              are not conversations: one is a diagnostic feed and the other is a file, and both
              stay the flat block they have always been.

              Siblings in a Panel rather than one control reshaped, because they are different
              shapes with different rules. Sharing a scroll viewer is what keeps following,
              the ↓ Newest button and the search offsets one mechanism rather than two.
            -->
            <Panel>
            <!-- No Text binding: the content is written as runs from code-behind, because a
                 marked line - the panel noting that the core changed - is drawn differently from
                 the conversation around it, and one string cannot carry that.

                 SelectableTextBlock rather than TextBlock, and that is the whole of "the live log
                 is selectable and copyable" (list.md Phase 19): drag-selection across the text
                 and Ctrl+C are the control's own, so there is no workflow to invent. The item
                 says so itself - "if it is free due to the nature of the controls being used,
                 then don't invent a workflow that's unneeded". SelectableTextBlockTests asserts
                 it stays that way. -->
            <SelectableTextBlock x:Name="Transcript"
                                 FontFamily="Cascadia Mono,Consolas,monospace"
                                 FontSize="{StaticResource D47.Type.Body}"
                                 TextWrapping="Wrap">
              <!--
                The other way to the same act (remediation.md 11, item 14). A right-click is where
                a reader looks for "clear this", and Ctrl+L is where their hands already are; the
                menu is also the only one of the two that says the shortcut exists.

                It clears the page and not the record — the model's own history is the turn loop's
                and the log file is Serilog's, and neither is this.
              -->
              <SelectableTextBlock.ContextMenu>
                <ContextMenu>
                  <!--
                    Copy of the selection (remediation.md 14, item 9). Declaring a menu of our own
                    replaced the one the control comes with, which is where a reader's hand goes
                    for Copy and where it had stopped being — so the shortcut that still worked
                    had nothing telling anybody it did. Greyed rather than absent when there is
                    nothing selected or nowhere to copy to, so the gesture is discoverable at the
                    moment it cannot be used as well as the moment it can.
                  -->
                  <MenuItem x:Name="CopySelectionItem"
                            Header="Copy"
                            IsEnabled="False"
                            InputGesture="Ctrl+C"
                            Click="OnCopySelectionClick" />
                  <Separator />
                  <MenuItem x:Name="ClearTranscriptItem"
                            Header="Clear what is shown"
                            InputGesture="Ctrl+L"
                            Click="OnClearTranscriptClick" />
                </ContextMenu>
              </SelectableTextBlock.ContextMenu>
            </SelectableTextBlock>

            <!--
              The conversation. Built from code-behind like the block beside it and for the same
              reason - who spoke decides how a turn is drawn, and one string cannot carry that.
              Empty and hidden until the page asks for it, so a surface reading the log file
              pays nothing for a conversation it is not showing.
            -->
            <StackPanel x:Name="Bubbles" IsVisible="False" />
            </Panel>
          </ScrollViewer>

          <!--
            The journal (https://github.com/dseelinger/d47/issues/51). A sibling of the scroller
            above rather than a third presentation inside it, because it is the one page here with
            a shape of its own: a list and a detail beside it, each scrolling separately.

            Design C of the four proposed, and the two panes answer each other's weakness — which
            is the whole argument for it. The summary line is prose, so it is lossy and capable of
            being wrong; the detail is the untouched fields, so it is complete and cannot be. An
            event nobody wrote a sentence for still lists, and its detail is exactly as complete as
            any other's.

            DrillView draws this shape already and is deliberately not used: it is a tab-level
            construct built around a navigation trail, and the Transcript tab has never had one.
            Borrowing it would mean giving this tab a drill stack it has no levels for.
          -->
          <Grid x:Name="JournalPane" IsVisible="False" ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto,1.2*">
            <ScrollViewer Grid.Column="0" x:Name="JournalListScroller">
              <ListBox x:Name="JournalList"
                       Background="Transparent"
                       BorderThickness="0"
                       FontFamily="Cascadia Mono,Consolas,monospace"
                       FontSize="{StaticResource D47.Type.Secondary}"
                       SelectionChanged="OnJournalSelected" />
            </ScrollViewer>

            <!--
              The same rule between panes the drill strip draws, and draggable for the same reason
              Phase 55 made those draggable. In its own column here rather than overlaid, because
              this grid is built once and never rebuilt by a navigation - the trap that phase names
              does not exist on this page.
            -->
            <GridSplitter Grid.Column="1"
                          x:Name="JournalSplitter"
                          Width="9"
                          Background="Transparent"
                          ResizeDirection="Columns"
                          ResizeBehavior="PreviousAndNext" />

            <Border Grid.Column="1"
                    Width="1"
                    HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                    IsHitTestVisible="False"
                    Background="{DynamicResource D47.Border}" />

            <ScrollViewer Grid.Column="2" x:Name="JournalDetailScroller" Padding="14,0,0,0">
              <!--
                Selectable, because the reason to open this pane at all is usually to put a field
                into a bug report. Same control and the same free copy the live log gets.
              -->
              <SelectableTextBlock x:Name="JournalDetail"
                                   FontFamily="Cascadia Mono,Consolas,monospace"
                                   FontSize="{StaticResource D47.Type.Secondary}"
                                   TextWrapping="NoWrap" />
            </ScrollViewer>
          </Grid>

          <!--
            Floating over the transcript rather than docked beside it, because it is about where
            the reader is rather than about the page: it appears only when there is something
            below the fold and takes no layout when there is not (list.md Phase 19, "Follow the
            live log, or stop following it").

            Hidden while following, which is the state a panel beside a running game is in
            almost all the time. A permanent "jump to latest" on a view that is already at the
            latest is a control that does nothing, sitting over the text it does nothing to.
          -->
          <Button x:Name="FollowButton"
                  IsVisible="False"
                  HorizontalAlignment="Right"
                  VerticalAlignment="Bottom"
                  Margin="0,0,14,10"
                  Padding="10,5"
                  CornerRadius="14"
                  FontSize="{StaticResource D47.Type.Secondary}"
                  Background="{DynamicResource D47.SurfaceAlt}"
                  BorderBrush="{DynamicResource D47.Border}"
                  BorderThickness="1"
                  ToolTip.Tip="Scroll to the newest line and follow it again"
                  Click="OnFollowClick" />
        </Panel>
      </Border>

      <!--
        Whichever furnished tab is showing, filled on its first selection by whatever the host
        handed to Furnish and never at all on a surface that was handed nothing. One pane rather
        than one per tab, with the built pages kept: constructing ninety-odd settings rows is not
        free, and a Commander who flips between Checklist and Settings should pay for each once.

        The fill is D47.Background rather than the transcript's D47.Surface because the cards
        inside a settings page are Surface, and a card the same colour as the page it sits on is
        not a card - which is also why the Settings tab has a control theme of its own for the
        join. A page that wants the transcript's fill draws its own.
      -->
      <Border x:Name="PagePane"
              IsVisible="False"
              ClipToBounds="True" />

      <!--
        The mini panel's reading of the tab that is showing (asked for 2026-08-22). Mini has no tab
        strip and cannot afford one at 512x280, so which tab it is reading is chosen on the big
        panel and this draws the short version of it. The transcript's tail is still the default and
        is still drawn by TranscriptPane; this pane is what Adventures looks like at that size, and
        the other tabs are unchanged until somebody asks for them.
      -->
      <Border x:Name="MiniPane"
              IsVisible="False"
              ClipToBounds="True" />

      <!--
        A chooser, or a value being said or typed. It takes the whole content region because that
        is what makes it a level of the stack rather than a popup - and because popups cannot
        exist in the VR path at all, where the panel's window is never shown and a popup has
        nowhere to hang from (list.md Phase 25, "Choosing takes the panel").

        Over the pane rather than inside it, so the tab underneath keeps its state: a chooser
        opened three levels into a ship comes back to that ship rather than to its root.
      -->
      <Border x:Name="ModalPane"
              IsVisible="False"
              ClipToBounds="True"
              Background="{DynamicResource D47.Background}"
              BorderBrush="{DynamicResource D47.Border}"
              BorderThickness="1"
              CornerRadius="0,4,4,4" />

      <!--
        The in-tree layer, for the choosers that are declared as layers rather than as pages - a
        five-item settings enum, where taking the whole panel would be a level of navigation to
        answer a question the Commander did not think of as one.

        Still not a popup. It is a panel in this tree, so the geometric hit test the headset uses
        finds it exactly as it finds everything else.
      -->
      <Panel x:Name="Layer" IsVisible="False" />
      </Panel>

      </DockPanel>
    </Border>

  </DockPanel>
</UserControl>
